For the existing local Fetch tests, wrap the real Fetch service:

```ts
const seen = yield* Queue.make<globalThis.RequestInit | undefined>();
const nativeFetch = yield* FetchHttpClient.Fetch;

const observingFetch: typeof globalThis.fetch = (input, init) => {
  Queue.offerUnsafe(seen, init);
  return nativeFetch(input, init);
};

yield* operation.pipe(
  Effect.provideService(FetchHttpClient.Fetch, observingFetch)
);

const init = yield* Queue.take(seen);
expect(init?.signal?.aborted).toBe(true);
```

Capture `nativeFetch` before providing the wrapper. This returns its existing Promise unchanged and needs no extra Effect runtime.

My synthetic, memory-only probe used `Promise.resolve(response)`. A synthetic Fetch replacement requires that Promise shape.

Your current `HttpClient.make` tests already exercise the installed client’s real abort signal and `fromWeb` response handling. They directly cover both fixes; adding the wrapper to the existing local server tests also covers the Fetch adapter. No source edits or checks run.

